{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794445496,"nonce":"/NavxJ8MosPwbz+I9Lemb9kX6VI=","title":"Fast frames place a nested binding's box and shadow wrongly","message":"Found by the animation-path harness (scenes \"motion nested\" and \"motion nested, no text\"), driven by spring targets so the compositor takes its binding fast path. An outer motion container translates, the inner one scales and turns about its own centre, which the outer one has moved.\n\nAgainst a full paint of the same frame, from the second step on: about 10300 px differ, channel difference 232, growing as the outer value moves. The difference is the inner box and its shadow, not a ghost of an earlier position. The same shapes with one binding each (translate, scale, rotate, fade) match a full paint to within 13 levels, so this is the interaction: the CPU patcher post-multiplies each binding's delta onto the primitives of its range, with an inherited-shift for ancestors' translation, and the inner range's pivot comes out wrong once the outer one has moved. The node table (122c606) composes the chain instead, so it is held to the full paint and not to this path.\n\nContact sheet: BLINC_ANIM_PATHS_OUT=\u003cdir\u003e. Listed in KNOWN_DIFFERENT in the harness.","files":null}]}